  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the biceps do?",
      "a": "The biceps is the muscle on the front of your upper arm. When it contracts it pulls on the forearm and bends your arm at the elbow. It also helps turn your palm up.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the biceps"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the triceps do?",
      "a": "The triceps is the muscle on the back of your upper arm. When it contracts it straightens your arm at the elbow, the opposite of the biceps.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the triceps"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why do muscles work in pairs?",
      "a": "Muscles can only pull, not push. So for each movement there is usually another muscle that pulls the opposite way to undo it, like the biceps bending the arm and the triceps straightening it.",
      "asks": [
        "What are antagonistic muscles?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the elbow hinge?",
      "a": "The elbow is a hinge joint where the upper arm bone meets the two forearm bones. It lets the forearm swing up and down, like a door on its hinges.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the elbow hinge"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What joins muscles to bones?",
      "a": "Tendons, tough cords of collagen, attach muscles to bones. When a muscle contracts, its tendon pulls the bone.",
      "asks": [
        "How do muscles pull on bones?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The biceps has two heads attached at the shoulder; the triceps has three.",
    "Skeletal muscles can only pull, so they are arranged in opposing pairs."
  ],
